  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Writing our first function in terms of $y,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=2x+2\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{1}{2}y-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $f(y)=\\frac{1}{2}y-1$ and $g(y)=\\sqrt{y-2}.$\n\nNow, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}y-1 & =\\sqrt{y-2} \\\\ y-2 & =2\\sqrt{y-2} \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =4(y-2) \\\\ (y-2)^{2}-4(y-2) & =0 \\\\ (y-2)((y-2)-4) & =0 \\\\ (y-2)(y-6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $y=2$ to $y=6.$\n\nLet's draw our horizontal arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14217745/assets/8e621715ded3c5ed527d2367785707939b1e3593707eaafe0b4c5e4509883e49.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $x=f(y)=\\frac{1}{2}y-1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $x=g(y)=\\sqrt{y-2}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{6}({[\\sqrt{y-2}]}^{2}-{[\\frac{1}{2}y-1]}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{6}(y-2-{[\\frac{1}{2}y-1]}^{2})\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
